-- title:
--   Transcendence of jmatĥ and finiteness of F₀ over K₀(jmatĥ), q=3
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $q = 3$, a nonzero level $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $A$; let $W$ be a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places (rational, affine geometric, with $j$-value in the supersingular set). Assume $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M' \le \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ via `hle`, and let $R_0$ be a constant reduction of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ relative to $A$ with values in the reduced field, whose residue map agrees coefficientwise with reduction of Laurent series over $A$. Further data: $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and $\pi \in A$; a primitive $q$-th root of unity index $\zeta$; families $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ of valuation subrings of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ indexed by $\mathbb P^1(\mathbb F_q)$ and $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$ indexed by $W$. The hypotheses on these families, summarised here, are: $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ at the line at infinity consists of the quotients $x/y$ of Laurent series with coefficients in $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction; each $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\ell)$ is the pullback of that ring along $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ moving the line at infinity to $\ell$; $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ is injective and its image is permuted by all such pullbacks; each $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ meets $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ exactly in $A$, is fixed by all $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, contains an element $t$ such that $t - a$ is a unit for every $a \in A$, and receives the elements of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ that are regular wherever $\hat\jmath$ is and whose residue is integral at $s$, compatibly with residues: for $a \in A$ reducing to the value of the residue at $s$, the difference lies in the maximal ideal of $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$. Finally, let $K_0$ be a subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ algebraic over it and $\pi \in K_0$; let $A_0$ be a henselian discrete valuation domain with an injective local ring homomorphism $\iota : A_0 \to A$ whose image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is $A \cap K_0$, inducing a surjection onto $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, with maximal ideal generated by $\varpi_0$ and $\iota(\varpi_0) = \pi$; let $F_0$ be the subfield of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ of those elements all of whose Laurent coefficients lie in $K_0$, containing the image $\hat\jmath$ of the $q$-expansion of $j$, and carrying an $A_0$-algebra structure whose structure map is $\iota$ followed by the inclusion of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. Then every polynomial $p$ over $A_0$ with $p(\hat\jmath) = 0$ is zero, and $F_0$ is finite-dimensional over the subfield of $F_0$ generated by the image of $A_0$ together with $\hat\jmath$.
--
--   This is the transcendence-and-finiteness step for the descended level field on the Igusa leg of the semistable covering construction at $q = 3$: it says that after descending coefficients to $K_0$, the modular invariant remains transcendental over the constants and the descended field is a finite extension of the rational function field it generates. It is the $q = 3$ counterpart of the corresponding statement for $q \ge 5$, and it is used in constructing the localised chart algebra at places outside the descent locus; its proof combines the bottom-layer admissible-constants comparison [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_admissible_smallConstants_botLayer_levelField_ringEquiv_of_descentBase_of_eq_three`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_admissible_smallConstants_botLayer_levelField_ringEquiv_of_descentBase_of_eq_three) with the general linear-disjointness statement [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.transcendental_and_finiteDimensional_adjoin_levelField`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.transcendental_and_finiteDimensional_adjoin_levelField).
